// The roadmap item 253 exit saga, run on a REAL Temporal dev server.
//
// Four crossings, chosen so one run proves three separate derived properties:
//
//   1. `settle` declares `idempotent(key: card)` — item 309's `keyed` register,
//      the one class Slice 2's derivation lets out of the at-most-once group.
//      Its host implementation fails once and then succeeds, so a live run that
//      reaches the end proves Temporal actually retried it.
//   2. `boom.trigger` declares nothing, so it stays at-most-once. Its host
//      implementation always fails, so the attempt count it records proves the
//      platform did NOT retry it — the property that keeps a non-idempotent
//      effect from being silently re-run.
//   3. `boom.trigger` failing aborts the saga, and the two compensations
//      registered before it must drain in the G7 LIFO order: refund, then
//      cancel. That order is DERIVED from source order, never authored.
service Flights { emission fn reserve(itinerary: Str) -> Str
                  emission fn cancel(key: Str) -> Str }
service Payments { emission fn charge(card: Str, total: Str) -> Str
                   emission fn refund(card: Str, total: Str) -> Str }
service Boom { emission fn trigger() -> Str }
extern emission idempotent(key: card) fn settle(card: Str, total: Str) -> Str
  = @ts { return card }
component ExitSaga requires flights: Flights, payments: Payments, boom: Boom {
  emit flights.reserve("ABC") compensate flights.cancel("ABC")
  emit payments.charge("visa", "100") compensate payments.refund("visa", "100")
  emit settle("visa", "100")
  emit boom.trigger()
}
// Every parameter is `Str` on purpose: revl's `Int` emits as a TS `bigint`,
// and Temporal's default payload converter is `JSON.stringify`, which throws
// on a BigInt. Carrying an `Int` across an activity boundary needs a custom
// data converter, which is its own piece of work and not what this saga is
// here to prove.
